/**
 * Rustic Cascader skin (WO-SKIN-02 checkpoint B, dropdown family).
 *
 * DELIBERATELY UNLAYERED, same rationale as every other engine skin (P-47).
 *
 * TWO SCOPE ROOTS. The trigger IS the root element -- there is no separate
 * wrapper -- and carries `.rottay-cascader.rottay-cascader--rustic
 * [data-part='trigger']` (the pre-step stamped `data-part='trigger'`, not
 * `'root'`; code is ground truth over the checkpoint brief's generic
 * "[data-part='root']" phrasing, same situation as TreeSelect rustic). The
 * dropdown is PORTALED via `createPortal(document.body)` (import L21, used
 * L496/L639) to a standalone subtree carrying
 * `.rottay-cascader__dropdown[data-part='dropdown']` -- everything inside it
 * (search input, menu columns, option rows, checkbox-free rows, empty state,
 * loading spinner) is OUTSIDE the trigger's DOM subtree, so those rules are
 * standalone, never `[data-part='trigger'] …` descendants (ARC-09 precedent,
 * matches TreeSelect rustic's portal posture exactly).
 *
 * SPECIFICITY (P-48): the Platform-only Rottay artifact ships an unlayered `*` border floor at
 * (0,3,1); a rule that sets a border COLOR (incl. `transparent`, excl.
 * `none`/`0`) must reach (0,4,0).
 *   - TRIGGER is a single element with 2 classes + 1 `[data-part]` attr ->
 *     (0,3,0) at its own selector, one short of the floor. The base
 *     border-color rule buys the 4th unit by repeating `[data-part='trigger']`;
 *     every state override (`[data-status='error'|'warning']`,
 *     `[data-open='true']:not([data-status])`) already carries a second
 *     attribute/pseudo of its own and reaches (0,4,0) naturally, so none of
 *     them needs the repeat trick. The rules are mutually exclusive (status
 *     vs. no-status), so declaration order does not matter for the cascade.
 *   - DROPDOWN (portal root) has 1 class + 1 attr -> (0,2,0); its descendants
 *     add one more attr -> (0,3,0), also one short. Any border-color
 *     declaration without an extra distinguishing attribute/pseudo
 *     (search-input-wrapper's border-bottom, search-input's rest-state
 *     border-color, the loading spinner's border, the option row's base
 *     transparent border-left) buys the 4th unit by repeating its own
 *     `[data-part='x']` -- following the Select-rustic/TreeSelect-rustic
 *     convention of splitting a background/border-color pair into two
 *     declarations so only the border one needs the repeat. Rules that
 *     already carry a second attribute or pseudo-class (`:focus`,
 *     `[data-selected='true']`, `:hover:not(...):not(...)`) reach (0,4,0)
 *     naturally and are left unrepeated.
 *
 * INTERACTION MECHANISM -- three independent imperative sites, all deleted:
 *   1. Trigger border color: `getBorderColor()` (error > warning > focus >
 *      default) fed a template-literal `border` shorthand. `isFocused` was
 *      local state set ONLY inside `handleOpenChange` as
 *      `setIsFocused(newOpen)` -- i.e. it always mirrored `isOpen` exactly, no
 *      other setter existed. Both `isFocused` state and `getBorderColor()`
 *      are deleted from the component; the priority chain is reproduced via
 *      the trigger's pre-existing `data-status`/`data-open` attributes
 *      (same supersession as TreeSelect rustic's `isFocused`).
 *   2. Search-input focus/blur (onFocus/onBlur, L515-522): wrote
 *      `.style.borderColor`/`.style.boxShadow` with hardcoded `#1677ff`/
 *      `rgba(22, 119, 255, …)` literals. DELETED; replaced by
 *      `[data-part='search-input']:focus`. The `onBlur` reset values
 *      (`var(--ds-cascader-border)`, `boxShadow: 'none'`) are byte-identical
 *      to this file's rest-state rule for the same part -- NO rest-after-blur
 *      delta.
 *   3. Menu-item hover, both variants, both DELETED and replaced by one rule
 *      `[data-part='option']:hover:not([data-selected='true']):not([data-disabled='true'])`
 *      (the shared selector correctly serves both call sites below):
 *      - Search-mode row (onMouseEnter/onMouseLeave, L548-555): NO guard at
 *        all in source (search results are never selected/disabled) -- both
 *        handlers held ONLY paint writes, so both are deleted outright.
 *      - Column-mode row (onMouseEnter/onMouseLeave, L589-601): guard is
 *        `!option.disabled && !isSelected` on enter, `!isSelected` on leave
 *        (leaf paint-reset only, no independent effect since disabled rows
 *        never got the enter-paint in the first place). `handleOptionHover
 *        (option, colIndex)` in onMouseEnter is called UNCONDITIONALLY
 *        (outside the `if`, guards its own disabled-check internally) and is
 *        NON-paint (drives column expansion) -- it is KEPT, onMouseEnter
 *        reduces to `() => handleOptionHover(option, colIndex)`; onMouseLeave
 *        held only the paint reset and is deleted entirely.
 *
 * RE-STAMPED DATA-PART: same `data-last` addition as the modern sibling, for
 * the same reason (`menuStyle(colIndex, totalCols)`'s conditional
 * `borderRight`, "not the last column") -- `data-last={colIndex ===
 * activeColumns.length - 1 || undefined}` on `<ul data-part='menu-column'>`;
 * `menu-column` itself (contract-test-pinned) is unchanged.
 *
 * Hardcoded literals transcribed byte-exact from the source `var(--…, #hex)`
 * fallbacks: `#1677ff` (search-input `:focus` border-color, option-row
 * selected/hover border-left), `rgba(22, 119, 255, 0.15)` +
 * `rgba(22, 119, 255, 0.08)` (search-input `:focus` box-shadow).
 *
 * KEYFRAMES: `@keyframes rottay-cascader-spin` / `rottay-cascader-dropdown-in`,
 * previously a plain `<style>` tag rendered inside the portal (remounting on
 * every open), are moved here renamed `ds-cascader-spin` /
 * `ds-cascader-dropdown-in`; the `<style>` tag is deleted and the spinner's
 * and dropdown's inline `animation:` references (non-paint keys) are updated
 * to match.
 */

@keyframes ds-cascader-spin {
  from {
    transform: rotate(0deg);
  }
  to {
    transform: rotate(360deg);
  }
}
@keyframes ds-cascader-dropdown-in {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: scaleY(0.95) translateY(-4px);
  }
  to {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: scaleY(1) translateY(0);
  }
}

