/**
 * Modern Carousel skin (WO-SKIN-05 checkpoint D1).
 *
 * DELIBERATELY UNLAYERED (P-47): every `rottay-*` cascade layer sorts before
 * Tailwind's base layer, so a layered rule cannot paint a component at all.
 * Unlayered outranks preflight and still yields to a caller's inline `style`.
 *
 * SCOPE ROOT carries `rottay-carousel rottay-carousel--modern` (fresh mints,
 * grep-verified free) alongside DaisyUI's structural `carousel` class, which is
 * left in place: `engines/modern/theme.css:798` paints `border-radius` and
 * `overflow` through it uncontested, and border-radius is a preflight-SAFE channel,
 * so dropping the class would remove a radius that renders today.
 *
 * Every rule uses a CHILD combinator. `data-part` is a shared vocabulary and each
 * slide holds arbitrary consumer content, which may itself be a component that
 * stamps `slide`, `arrow` or `dot`.
 *
 * SPECIFICITY (P-48): the arrow's `border` shorthand carries a real colour, so it
 * must clear the tenant's unlayered `*` border-color floor at (0,3,1); the repeated
 * `[data-part='arrow']` buys the fourth unit. It stays a SHORTHAND because preflight
 * zeroes `border-width` on every element.
 *
 * The slide offset is arithmetic over the live slide index (`(index - currentSlide)
 * * 100%`), so it cannot be enumerated as a rule; it rides the
 * `--ds-carousel-slide-transform` custom property, set inline per slide. Its
 * fallback is `none`, the same value the fade branch produces.
 *
 * Values transcribed verbatim from `engines/modern.tsx`.
 */

.rottay-carousel.rottay-carousel--modern > [data-part='track'] > [data-part='slide'] {
  transform: var(--ds-carousel-slide-transform, none);
}

/* ---- arrows: both buttons carried the same object ---- */
.rottay-carousel.rottay-carousel--modern > [data-part='arrow'][data-part='arrow'] {
  background: var(--ds-surface-card);
  color: var(--ds-color-text-primary);
  border-radius: 50%;
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-color-border);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-elevation-1);
}

/* ---- dots ---- */
.rottay-carousel.rottay-carousel--modern > [data-part='dots'] > [data-part='dot'][data-selected='true'] {
  background: var(--ds-color-primary);
}
.rottay-carousel.rottay-carousel--modern > [data-part='dots'] > [data-part='dot'][data-selected='false'] {
  background: var(--ds-surface-panel);
}

/**
 * Rustic Carousel skin (WO-SKIN-05 checkpoint D1).
 *
 * DELIBERATELY UNLAYERED (P-47): no `rottay-*` layer can paint a component
 * (Tailwind's `@layer base` sorts after all of them). Unlayered outranks preflight
 * and still yields to the caller's inline `style`.
 *
 * SCOPE ROOT carries `rottay-carousel rottay-carousel--rustic` (fresh mints,
 * grep-verified free). This engine renders no DaisyUI class, so it takes none of
 * the `.carousel` chrome the modern engine's theme bridge paints — an asymmetry
 * that is preserved, not unified.
 *
 * Every rule uses a CHILD combinator: `data-part` is a shared vocabulary and each
 * slide holds arbitrary consumer content.
 *
 * SPECIFICITY (P-48): the arrow's `border` shorthand carries a real colour and must
 * clear the tenant's unlayered `*` border-color floor at (0,3,1); the repeated
 * `[data-part='arrow']` buys the fourth unit. The dot's `border: none` is a
 * colour-free reset and is exempt.
 *
 * Two transforms cannot be enumerated as rules and ride custom properties set
 * inline: the slide offset is arithmetic over the live slide index, and the dots
 * rail's centring transform is one branch of a positioning switch whose four other
 * declarations (top/left/bottom/right/flex-direction) are not paint and stay inline
 * with it. Splitting the transform away from its own switch would leave the two
 * halves of one decision in two files.
 *
 * Values transcribed verbatim from `engines/rustic.tsx`.
 */

.rottay-carousel.rottay-carousel--rustic > [data-part='track'] > [data-part='slide'] {
  transform: var(--ds-carousel-slide-transform, none);
}

/* ---- arrows: one shared object, positioned per side by an inline left/right ---- */
.rottay-carousel.rottay-carousel--rustic > [data-part='arrow'][data-part='arrow'] {
  transform: translateY(-50%);
  background: var(--ds-carousel-arrow-bg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9));
  border: 1px solid var(--ds-carousel-arrow-border, #ddd);
  border-radius: var(--ds-carousel-arrow-radius, 50%);
}

/* ---- dots rail ---- */
.rottay-carousel.rottay-carousel--rustic > [data-part='dots'] {
  transform: var(--ds-carousel-dots-transform);
}

/* ---- dots ---- */
.rottay-carousel.rottay-carousel--rustic > [data-part='dots'] > [data-part='dot'] {
  border-radius: var(--ds-carousel-dot-radius, 4px);
  border: none;
}
.rottay-carousel.rottay-carousel--rustic > [data-part='dots'] > [data-part='dot'][data-selected='true'] {
  background: var(--ds-carousel-dot-active-bg, var(--ds-color-primary-500, #1890ff));
}
.rottay-carousel.rottay-carousel--rustic > [data-part='dots'] > [data-part='dot'][data-selected='false'] {
  background: var(--ds-carousel-dot-bg, #d9d9d9);
}

/**
 * Modern Collapse skin (WO-SKIN-05 checkpoint L).
 *
 * DELIBERATELY UNLAYERED (P-47): every `rottay-*` layer sorts before
 * Tailwind's `base` layer, so a layered rule loses to preflight regardless
 * of specificity. Unlayered outranks preflight and still yields to a
 * caller's inline `style`.
 *
 * SCOPE ROOT keeps the component's existing bare `rottay-collapse` class
 * (plus conditional `--ghost`/`--borderless` modifiers) -- this is the SAME
 * class `engines/modern/theme.css:654-671`'s live bridge already targets on
 * `border-color`/`border-radius` (a real, measured 14px radius today per the
 * WO-SKIN-05 orchestrator adjudication A1). An unlayered rule anchored to
 * that class outranks the layered bridge outright, so nothing here needs to
 * out-fight it; it simply wins going forward the same way every other
 * unlayered skin already does. Rustic's Collapse could NOT reuse this class
 * for the identical reason in reverse (it would hand rustic a radius it
 * never had) -- see `engines/rustic/skin/collapse.css`.
 *
 * SPECIFICITY (P-48): the only border-color-bearing rule here is the
 * bordered panel (below) -- the two root classes + `[data-part='root']`
 * already reach (0,3,0), and the descendant `[data-part='panel']` repeated
 * buys the 4th unit. Every other declaration (background, box-shadow,
 * border-radius, color, transform) is non-border and clears preflight
 * unlayered at (0,3,0) or better. Values transcribed verbatim from
 * `engines/modern.tsx`.
 */

/* ---- arrow: rotation (was the inline `transform` ternary on `isActive`). ---- */
.rottay-collapse[data-part='root'] [data-part='arrow'] {
  transform: rotate(0deg);
}
.rottay-collapse[data-part='root'] [data-part='arrow'][data-expanded='true'] {
  transform: rotate(90deg);
}

/* ---- panel: border-radius + surface (was the Panel root's inline style).
        Ghost overrides both to its own token pair; the extra
        `.rottay-collapse--ghost` class naturally out-specifies the default
        rule below, no guard needed since only two mutually distinct states
        exist. ---- */
.rottay-collapse[data-part='root'] [data-part='panel'] {
  border-radius: var(--ds-collapse-root-default-idle-border-radius, var(--ds-radius-md));
  background: var(--ds-collapse-root-default-idle-bg, var(--ds-surface-card));
  box-shadow: var(--ds-collapse-root-default-idle-shadow, none);
}
.rottay-collapse.rottay-collapse--ghost[data-part='root'] [data-part='panel'] {
  border-radius: var(--ds-collapse-root-ghost-idle-border-radius, 0);
  background: var(--ds-collapse-root-ghost-idle-bg, transparent);
  box-shadow: var(--ds-collapse-root-ghost-idle-shadow, none);
}

/* ---- panel: border, bordered case only (was the `context.bordered` spread
        -- when not bordered, no border key was ever set, so no rule fires
        for that state). `:not(.rottay-collapse--borderless)` is both the
        gate for `bordered` (the two are complementary, see
        `Collapse.types.ts`'s COLLAPSE_DEFAULTS) and the specificity unit
        that clears the (0,4,0) border-color floor. ---- */
.rottay-collapse:not(.rottay-collapse--borderless)[data-part='root'] [data-part='panel'][data-part='panel'] {
  border: var(--ds-collapse-root-default-idle-border-width, 1px) var(--ds-collapse-root-default-idle-border-style, solid) var(--ds-collapse-root-default-idle-border-color, var(--ds-color-border));
}

/* ---- header: color, 3-way priority (disabled > expanded > idle). Mutually
        exclusive `:not()` guards reproduce the JS ternary's priority order
        without relying on cascade position. ---- */
.rottay-collapse[data-part='root'] [data-part='header'] {
  color: var(--ds-collapse-header-default-idle-color, inherit);
}
.rottay-collapse[data-part='root'] [data-part='header'][data-expanded='true']:not([data-disabled='true']) {
  color: var(--ds-collapse-header-default-expanded-color, var(--ds-color-primary));
}
.rottay-collapse[data-part='root'] [data-part='header'][data-disabled='true'] {
  color: var(--ds-collapse-header-default-disabled-color, var(--ds-color-text-disabled));
}

/* ---- header: background, 4-way priority (disabled > expanded > ghost > idle).
        Same mutually-exclusive-guard idiom; the ghost rule additionally
        excludes expanded/disabled so an active ghost panel resolves to the
        expanded rule instead, matching the JS ternary's nesting order. ---- */
.rottay-collapse[data-part='root'] [data-part='header'] {
  background: var(--ds-collapse-header-default-idle-bg, transparent);
}
.rottay-collapse.rottay-collapse--ghost[data-part='root'] [data-part='header']:not([data-expanded='true']):not([data-disabled='true']) {
  background: var(--ds-collapse-header-ghost-idle-bg, transparent);
}
.rottay-collapse[data-part='root'] [data-part='header'][data-expanded='true']:not([data-disabled='true']) {
  background: var(--ds-collapse-header-default-expanded-bg, transparent);
}
.rottay-collapse[data-part='root'] [data-part='header'][data-disabled='true'] {
  background: var(--ds-collapse-header-default-disabled-bg, transparent);
}

/* ---- content-inner: color (was a STATIC, unconditional inline value, no
        ternary in the source) + background (was the `context.ghost`
        ternary). ---- */
.rottay-collapse[data-part='root'] [data-part='content-inner'] {
  color: var(--ds-collapse-content-default-idle-color, inherit);
  background: var(--ds-collapse-content-default-idle-bg, transparent);
}
.rottay-collapse.rottay-collapse--ghost[data-part='root'] [data-part='content-inner'] {
  background: var(--ds-collapse-content-ghost-idle-bg, transparent);
}

/**
 * Rustic Collapse skin (WO-SKIN-05 checkpoint L).
 *
 * DELIBERATELY UNLAYERED (P-47), same rationale as every other engine skin:
 * a layered rule loses to Tailwind's `base` layer regardless of
 * specificity; unlayered outranks preflight and still yields to a caller's
 * inline `style`.
 *
 * SCOPE ROOT mints `rottay-collapse-shell rottay-collapse-shell--rustic`
 * (grep-verified free across CSS and CSS-in-JS). Per the WO-SKIN-05
 * orchestrator adjudication A1, rustic must NOT reuse modern's bare
 * `rottay-collapse` -- `engines/modern/theme.css:654-671` declares a live
 * bridge on that exact classname with NO `[data-engine]` gate, on the
 * preflight-SAFE `border-radius` channel (measured 14px). Reusing it here
 * would hand rustic a radius it has never painted. `rottay-collapse-shell`
 * mirrors the `rottay-alert-shell`/`rottay-progress-shell` precedent.
 *
 * `context.ghost`/`context.bordered` have NO DOM signal on this engine
 * (rustic's root/panel carry no ghost/bordered class or attribute, unlike
 * modern's `--ghost`/`--borderless` root modifiers) -- inventing one would
 * be new `data-*` anatomy the WO-SKIN-05 pre-step did not stamp. The border
 * channel (the one place ghost truly changes the painted value: `none` vs a
 * real bordered color) rides the `--ds-collapse-panel-border` custom-property
 * hatch instead, set inline to the exact resolved value every render; this
 * skin only supplies the `var()` fallback. Background does NOT need the
 * hatch: `styles.panel` sets no background at all (bordered case) and
 * `styles.panelGhost.backgroundColor` is `'transparent'` (ghost case) --
 * both branches resolve to the same rendered value, so one unconditional
 * `transparent` rule reproduces both without any conditional at all.
 *
 * SPECIFICITY (P-48): the border rule paints a real color in the bordered
 * branch and must reach (0,4,0) -- the two root classes + `[data-part='root']`
 * already give (0,3,0), and the descendant `[data-part='panel']` repeated
 * buys the 4th unit. Every other declaration here is non-border. Values
 * transcribed verbatim from `engines/rustic.tsx`.
 */

/* ---- panel: border-radius (was styles.panel.borderRadius, unconditional --
        panelGhost never overrides it) + background (was styles.panel's
        absent backgroundColor / styles.panelGhost's 'transparent': the same
        rendered value either way, see header). ---- */
.rottay-collapse-shell.rottay-collapse-shell--rustic[data-part='root'] [data-part='panel'] {
  border-radius: var(--ds-collapse-radius, 12px);
  background-color: transparent;
}

/* ---- panel: border (was styles.panel.border / styles.panelGhost's 'none'
        override, driven by `context.ghost` -- no DOM signal, so the resolved
        value rides `--ds-collapse-panel-border`, always set inline). ---- */
