/**
 * Rustic Timeline skin (WO-SKIN-05 checkpoint D2).
 *
 * DELIBERATELY UNLAYERED (P-47): a layered rule sorts before Tailwind's base
 * layer and loses to preflight; unlayered outranks preflight and still yields to
 * the caller's inline `style`.
 *
 * SCOPE ROOT carries `rottay-timeline rottay-timeline--rustic` +
 * `data-part='root'`. The engine suffix is load-bearing: modern carries the same
 * base class.
 *
 * THE DOT COLOR CANNOT BE ENUMERATED. Rustic resolves an item's color through
 * `TIMELINE_COLOR_MAP[color] || color` -- an unmapped value passes straight
 * through, so `<Timeline.Item color="#ff0000">` is a supported call and the dot
 * fill is genuinely runtime data, not a closed enum. It therefore rides the
 * `--ds-timeline-dot-color` custom property the component sets inline (a quoted
 * key, not a paint key) and is consumed here. Modern's map has no pass-through
 * branch, which is why its skin CAN key the fill on `data-tone`; the asymmetry
 * is real and transcribed, not levelled.
 *
 * `data-custom-dot` carries the branch that a caller-supplied dot element flips:
 * the default circle goes transparent and drops its ring so the custom content
 * shows through without overlap.
 *
 * The collision-safe `@keyframes rottay-timeline-pulse` driving the pending dot
 * now lives once in `components/skin/primitive-motion.css`; the animation name
 * and frame body remain unchanged.
 *
 * SPECIFICITY (P-48): the dot's `border` shorthand clears the tenant's unlayered
 * (0,3,1) `*` border-color floor at (0,5,0). Values transcribed verbatim from
 * `engines/rustic.tsx`.
 */

/* ---- the absolutely-positioned vertical line (geometry stays inline) ---- */
.rottay-timeline.rottay-timeline--rustic[data-part='root'] > [data-part='connector'] {
  background-color: var(--ds-timeline-line-color, #e8e8e8);
